<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanaya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nanaya was a Mesopotamian goddess of love and war, worshipped from the 3rd millennium BCE and closely linked with Inanna-Ishtar. Her cult spread east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, where she was venerated at temples in Elymais and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, and later as Nana among the Kushans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nanaya.jpg" alt="Nanaya" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
